g&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Michael_III_and_Basil_I_sb1693.jpg/500px-Michael_III_and_Basil_I_sb1693.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 9th century Byzantine coin showing emperor Michael III and his then-junior colleague Basil I with the Latin titles \"imperator\" and \"rex\", respectively. Although spoken Latin had largely disappeared in the Eastern Roman Empire by that time, it was occasionally still used on coinage and in ceremonies until at least the 11th century.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMichael_III_and_Basil_I_sb1693.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Calligraphy.malmesbury.bible.arp.jpg/500px-Calligraphy.malmesbury.bible.arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Calligraphy.malmesbury.bible.arp.jpg/1280px-Calligraphy.malmesbury.bible.arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Calligraphy.malmesbury.bible.arp.jpg/500px-Calligraphy.malmesbury.bible.arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Latin Malmesbury Bible, from 1407","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACalligraphy.malmesbury.bible.arp.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Incunabula_distribution_by_language.png/500px-Incunabula_distribution_by_language.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Incunabula_distribution_by_language.png/1280px-Incunabula_distribution_by_language.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Incunabula_distribution_by_language.png/500px-Incunabula_distribution_by_language.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Most 15th-century printed books (incunabula) were in Latin, with the vernacular languages playing only a secondary role.[22]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIncunabula_distribution_by_language.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Wallsend_platfom_2_02.jpg/500px-Wallsend_platfom_2_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Wallsend_platfom_2_02.jpg/500px-Wallsend_platfom_2_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The signs at Wallsend Metro station are in English and Latin, as a tribute to Wallsend's role as one of the outposts of the Roman Empire, as the eastern end of Hadrian's Wall (hence the name) at Segedunum.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWallsend_platfom_2_02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Commentarii_de_Bello_Gallico.jpg/500px-Commentarii_de_Bello_Gallico.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Commentarii_de_Bello_Gallico.jpg/500px-Commentarii_de_Bello_Gallico.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Julius Caesar's Commentarii de Bello Gallico is one of the most famous classical Latin texts of the Golden Age of Latin. The unvarnished, journalistic style of this patrician general has long been taught as a model of the urbane Latin officially spoken and written in the floruit of the Roman Republic.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACommentarii_de_Bello_Gallico.jpg"}],"sounds":{"charm":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/89/En-us-charm.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktio